/-
Mathathon Round 1, Problem 63: official Lean statement.

Written for Mathathon; not taken from an existing formalization.
It states that there is an infinite finitely presented nilsemigroup, answering a question of
L. N. Shevrin and M. V. Sapir (I. A. Ivanov-Pogodaev and A. Ya. Kanel-Belov: announced in
"Construction of infinite finitely presented nilsemigroup", Doklady Mathematics 101 (2020),
81–85, arXiv:1912.01695; full proof in Izv. Math. 85 (2021), Fundam. Prikl. Mat. 24 (2022)
and Algebra and Logic 63 (2025)). Their semigroup even satisfies the identity x⁹ = 0; the
statement below asks only for the property named on the problem page, that every element is
nilpotent. A semigroup is finitely presented if it is isomorphic to a free semigroup on
finitely many generators modulo the congruence generated by finitely many relations; its zero
is one of its elements, so no zero symbol needs to be added to the presentation.
-/

import Mathlib

namespace Mathathon.P63

/-- A semigroup `S` is finitely presented if, for some `k` and some finite set `R` of pairs of
words, `S` is isomorphic to the free semigroup on `k` generators modulo the congruence
generated by the relations `u = v` for `(u, v) ∈ R`. -/
def IsFinitelyPresented (S : Type) [Semigroup S] : Prop :=
  ∃ (k : ℕ) (R : Finset (FreeSemigroup (Fin k) × FreeSemigroup (Fin k))),
    Nonempty ((conGen fun u v => (u, v) ∈ R).Quotient ≃* S)

/-- An element `x` of a semigroup with zero is nilpotent if some positive power of `x` is zero.
Here `(x * ·)^[n] x` is the power `x ^ (n + 1) = x * ⋯ * x` (`n + 1` factors). -/
def IsNil {S : Type} [SemigroupWithZero S] (x : S) : Prop :=
  ∃ n : ℕ, (x * ·)^[n] x = 0

/-- **Infinite finitely presented nilsemigroup.** There is a finitely presented semigroup with
zero that is infinite and in which every element is nilpotent. -/
theorem exists_infinite_finitely_presented_nilsemigroup :
    ∃ (S : Type) (_ : SemigroupWithZero S),
      IsFinitelyPresented S ∧ Infinite S ∧ ∀ x : S, IsNil x := by
  sorry

end Mathathon.P63
